talk(356,'1975-03-03','V. N. Tulovskii','P000589-P000594','Oscillatory integrals and Lagrangian distributions',r"""The dated session occupies P000590--P000592, ending before 10 March. It constructs oscillatory distributions, their conic Lagrangians, and Fourier integral kernels. Kirillov inserts the affine geometry of Lagrangian leaves. Except for that intervention, the explicit reference remains Hörmander's already published FIO theory. The corollary about possible leaf topologies is too strong.""")
item(356,'P000590','theorem','Testing wavefront regularity with nonlinear phases',r"""The linear Fourier phase is replaced by \(\Phi\) with \(\Phi'(x_0)=\xi_0\), and the pairing with \(\varphi e^{i\lambda\Phi}\) is rapidly decreasing.""",r"""Fix \(\widehat u(\xi)=\langle u,e^{-ix\cdot\xi}\rangle\). Microlocal smoothness at \((x_0,\xi_0)\) is equivalent to rapid decay of \(\langle u,\chi e^{-it\psi}\rangle\) as \(t\to+\infty\), for cutoffs supported sufficiently near \(x_0\) and smooth real phases whose differentials stay in a sufficiently small cone around \(\xi_0\). The estimates are uniform over suitable bounded families of such phases and cutoffs.""",r"""This is the nonlinear phase form of the Fourier criterion and nonstationary-phase estimates in \citet[Sections 2.3 and 2.5]{hormander1971fourier}. The notebook's plus sign is consistent with the opposite Fourier convention; its missing local and uniform quantifiers require repair.""",[H],reconstruction='A single negative-sign Fourier convention is used here and below; the original convention is not specified.',match='partial')
item(356,'P000590-P000591','construction','Defining an oscillatory distribution by cutoff removal',r"""For \(a\in S^m_{1,0}\), \(\langle A,u\rangle=\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\int a(x,\theta)e^{i\Phi(x,\theta)}u(x)\chi(\varepsilon\theta)\,dx\,d\theta\).""",r"""Let \(\Phi\) be smooth real and positively homogeneous of degree one for \(\theta\ne0\), with \(d_{x,\theta}\Phi\ne0\), and \(a\in S^m_{1,0}\), with harmless smooth extension at bounded \(\theta\). For a smooth compactly supported cutoff \(\chi=1\) near zero, integration by parts defines the indicated distributional limit independently of \(\chi\). Tests are compactly supported in the \(x\)-variables.""",r"""\citet[Section 1.2 and Proposition 2.5.7]{hormander1971fourier} contains this construction; compare 1973-329.12--13. Homogeneity and nonvanishing phase differential are part of the phase-function definition, not optional hypotheses.""",[H],reconstruction='The meaning of phase function is supplied explicitly.')
item(356,'P000591','remark','The linear-phase delta example',r"""For \(a=1\), \(\Phi=\langle x,\theta\rangle\), the notebook gives \(\langle A,u\rangle=u(0)\).""",r"""With ordinary Lebesgue \(d\theta\) and the Fourier convention fixed above, \(\int_{\mathbb R^n}e^{ix\cdot\theta}\,d\theta=(2\pi)^n\delta_0\). Thus the stated value \(u(0)\) uses the normalized measure \((2\pi)^{-n}d\theta\). The distribution is concentrated on the critical set \(x=0\).""",r"""This is Fourier inversion, the basic example behind \citet[Section 1.2]{hormander1971fourier}. The notebook does not fix its measure normalization, so this is a convention clarification rather than a certain mathematical error.""",[H],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(356,'P000591','method','Removing the nonstationary part of an amplitude',r"""Split \(a=a_1+a_2\) near a point where \(\Phi'_\theta\ne0\); the corresponding \(A_2\) is smooth.""",r"""On a closed conic support where \(|\partial_\theta\Phi|\) is bounded away from zero after angular localization, choose a differential operator \(L\) in \(\theta\) with \(Le^{i\Phi}=e^{i\Phi}\). Iterating its transpose improves amplitude integrability and proves that the resulting oscillatory integral is smooth in \(x\).""",r"""The nonstationary-phase integration-by-parts method is established in \citet[Section 1.2]{hormander1971fourier}. The conic cutoff in the sketch records localization, not an additional singularity.""",[H],literal='correct')
item(356,'P000591','theorem','The critical-set bound for the wavefront',r"""\(C_\Phi=\{\Phi'_\theta=0\}\), \(\lambda_\Phi(x,\theta)=(x,\Phi'_x)\), and \(\mathrm{WF}(A)\subset\Lambda_\Phi=\lambda_\Phi(C_\Phi)\).""",r"""For a phase and symbol as above, the wavefront of its oscillatory integral is contained in the closed conic set of \((x,\partial_x\Phi(x,\theta))\) with \(\partial_\theta\Phi=0\) and \((x,\theta)\) in the conic support of the amplitude. One takes the relevant closure when supports or charts are not proper.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 2.5.7]{hormander1971fourier} is the exact analytic source. The formula is a containment; amplitudes may vanish or cancel, so equality is not automatic.""",[H],literal='correct')
item(356,'P000591','definition','Nondegenerate phases parametrize Lagrangians',r"""The rank of \((\Phi''_{\theta x},\Phi''_{\theta\theta})\) is \(N\) on \(C_\Phi\); then \(\lambda_\Phi\) is locally a diffeomorphism and \(\Lambda_\Phi\) is conic Lagrangian.""",r"""If the \(N\) differentials \(d(\partial_{\theta_j}\Phi)\) are independent on \(C_\Phi\), this critical set is smooth of dimension \(n\). The map \(\lambda_\Phi\) is a conic Lagrangian immersion and locally identifies \(C_\Phi\) with its image. It is not a local diffeomorphism onto the \(2n\)-dimensional cotangent bundle.""",r"""This is the standard nondegenerate phase construction in \citet[Section 3.1]{hormander1971fourier}. The notebook's local-diffeomorphism phrase is correct only with the image as target.""",[H])
item(356,'P000591','theorem','Local phase functions for every conic Lagrangian',r"""Conversely every smooth conic Lagrangian can locally be obtained from a nondegenerate phase, and one may take \(N=n\).""",r"""Every germ of a smooth conic Lagrangian in \(T^*X\setminus0\), \(\dim X=n\), has a local positively homogeneous nondegenerate phase representation with at most \(n\) phase variables. Additional variables may be introduced by the usual stabilization on a conic chart, with the accompanying amplitude-order shift.""",r"""\citet[Section 3.1, especially Theorems 3.1.3 and 3.1.6]{hormander1971fourier} establishes local parametrization and phase equivalence. The statement is local in the Lagrangian, not a claim of one global generating function.""",[H],literal='correct')
item(356,'P000591','method','Euler homogeneity proves the Lagrangian condition',r"""On \(C_\Phi\), \(\Phi'_\theta=0\) implies \(\Phi=0\), then \(\Phi'_x\,dx=0\); hence \(\xi\,dx=0\) on the image.""",r"""Euler's identity gives \(\Phi=\theta\cdot\partial_\theta\Phi=0\) on \(C_\Phi\). Restricting \(d\Phi\) gives \(\partial_x\Phi\,dx=0\), so the pullback of the Liouville form vanishes. Its exterior derivative therefore vanishes too; together with nondegeneracy and dimension \(n\), the image is Lagrangian.""",r"""This is the elementary proof in the phase geometry of \citet[Section 3.1]{hormander1971fourier}. Homogeneity is essential to the stronger vanishing of the Liouville form.""",[H],literal='correct')
item(356,'P000591','theorem','Equivalent phases define the same distribution class',r"""Two phases parametrizing the same \(\Lambda\) produce the same class of \(A\).""",r"""The microlocal class \(I^m(X,\Lambda)\) is independent of the chosen nondegenerate parametrization. In Hörmander's normalization a phase with \(N\) variables uses amplitude order \(m+(\dim X-2N)/4\). Changing \(N\) changes that amplitude order; the principal symbol also includes the stationary-phase signature/Maslov and density factors.""",r"""\citet[Theorem 3.2.1 and Definition 3.2.2]{hormander1971fourier} supplies the exact phase-invariance theorem. Equal raw amplitude orders for phases with different \(N\) would be incorrect.""",[H],reconstruction='The order shift suppressed in the notebook is restored.')
item(356,'P000591-P000592','construction','Fourier integral kernels and gluing over phase charts',r"""For \(X,Y\), a conic Lagrangian in \(T^*(X\times Y)\setminus0\) gives a kernel by an oscillatory integral; in general use a sum of phase charts.""",r"""A Fourier integral kernel is a Lagrangian distribution on \(X\times Y\), locally represented by nondegenerate oscillatory integrals with compatible order. Partitions of unity and locally finite sums produce the global distribution. To extend the resulting operator to compactly supported distribution inputs, exclude the input axis wavefront; proper support controls noncompact inputs.""",r"""This is the FIO construction of \citet[Sections 3.2 and 4.1]{hormander1971fourier}. The associated operator relation reverses the sign of the \(Y\)-covector, as already recorded in 1974-353.9.""",[H])
item(356,'P000592','theorem','Kirillov: affine structures on Lagrangian leaves',r"""If a symplectic manifold has a Lagrangian foliation, its leaves carry an invariantly defined affine structure.""",r"""For a Lagrangian foliation with tangent bundle \(L\), the symplectic form identifies \(L\) with its annihilator in \(T^*M\). Transporting the Bott partial connection on that annihilator gives a flat torsion-free connection along each leaf. This is an intrinsic affine structure, without a completeness or trivial-holonomy conclusion.""",r"""A checked modern formulation and construction are \citet[Proposition 1.9 and Theorem 1.10]{loustausanders2017bilagrangian}, whose introduction attributes the result to Weinstein (1971). The 1971 original was not accessible for direct theorem verification; the modern result was read. The notebook is therefore an expository occurrence, not evidence of priority for Kirillov.""",[LS],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',match='exact',confidence='medium')
item(356,'P000592','construction','Basic Hamiltonians give parallel commuting fields',r"""For functions constant along the leaves, the covectors \(d\varphi\) correspond to parallel vector fields; these commute.""",r"""If \(f\) and \(g\) are locally constant along a Lagrangian foliation, their Hamiltonian fields are tangent to its leaves and \(\{f,g\}=0\); hence they commute. Locally independent basic differentials give a parallel frame for the leafwise affine connection. Such a frame need not be globally single-valued or complete.""",r"""This is the Hamiltonian description accompanying \citet[Theorem 1.10]{loustausanders2017bilagrangian}. The notebook calls the vectors gradients, but the construction uses the symplectic isomorphism, not a Riemannian metric.""",[LS],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',literal='correct',match='partial')
item(356,'P000592','claim','The asserted Euclidean-or-torus leaf dichotomy',r"""\ru{Следствие: слои --- \(\mathbb R^n\) или торы}. An uncertain abbreviation follows as an example of a Lagrangian foliation.""",r"""The dichotomy is false. For \(T^*(S^1\times\mathbb R)\) with the standard form, the leaves with both momenta constant are Lagrangian cylinders \(S^1\times\mathbb R\). An affine structure alone gives no such topological classification. A connected leaf with \(n\) globally independent complete commuting fields is instead \(\mathbb R^n/\Gamma\), with discrete \(\Gamma\), hence may have any cylinder rank.""",r"""The leafwise result verified in \citet[Theorem 1.10]{loustausanders2017bilagrangian} does not assert the notebook corollary. The editorial counterexample is checked in the mathematical audit. The following abbreviation likely denotes a cotangent bundle (\ru{кокасательное расслоение}); it should not be turned into a person named Kokas.""",[LS],speaker='A. A. Kirillov',literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='As of November 2026: the literal corollary is false; the affine-connection theorem is established, and no open classification problem is inferred.')
item(356,'P000592','claim','Recognizing FIOs by rapidly oscillating tests',r"""A kernel whose wavefront lies in \(\Lambda\) and which acts well on transverse oscillating exponentials is an FIO; an asymptotic series is sketched.""",r"""For a classical Lagrangian distribution of order \(m\) on a \(d\)-manifold, pairings with \(\chi e^{-it\psi}\), where \(\operatorname{graph}(d\psi)\) meets \(\Lambda\) transversely, have stationary-phase expansions with leading scale \(t^{m-d/4}\) and phase values at the intersections. A converse requires uniform symbol estimates in test-phase parameters, not merely a separate pointwise asymptotic for each \(\psi\).""",r"""\citet[Theorems 3.2.4--3.2.5 and the following characterization paragraph]{hormander1971fourier} establishes the forward statement and describes the converse. Record 357 repeats the question explicitly. The notebook suppresses the symbol order and essential uniformity.""",[H],match='partial',problem='GP-1974-21')
corr('P000591','Phase-parametrization local diffeomorphism',r"""\(\lambda_\Phi\) is a local diffeomorphism into \(T^*\Omega\setminus0\).""",r"""It is an immersion and a local diffeomorphism onto its Lagrangian image.""",'Dimension count: the critical set has dimension n and the cotangent target dimension 2n; the scan omits the image qualification.')
corr('P000592','Global type of Lagrangian leaves',r"""Leaves are \(\mathbb R^n\) or tori.""",r"""Leaves have a natural affine structure; neither completeness nor a global topological dichotomy follows.""",'The scan clearly states the dichotomy. The standard cylinder foliation is a direct counterexample.')
corr('P000592','Example after the leaf corollary','“Kokas.” construction.','Likely “cotangent bundle,” from the abbreviation for кокасательное расслоение; preserve uncertainty.','The scan resembles “Кокас. рассл.” The context is the standard fibrewise Lagrangian foliation of a cotangent bundle.','en','ambiguity')
save()

talk(357,'1975-03-10','V. N. Tulovskii','P000592-P000596','FIO normal forms, propagation, and characteristic dimension',r"""The session starts on P000593 and ends with Bernstein's short intervention on P000594. Tulovskii restates FIO kernels, a testing characterization, the normal form for a real principal symbol, and propagation of singularities. Gelfand asks about a \(p\)-adic version. Bernstein switches from the analytic wavefront of a distribution to the algebraic characteristic variety of a differential-operator ideal; that change of meaning is essential.""")
item(357,'P000593','construction','FIO operator action on compactly supported distributions',r"""The oscillatory kernel formula is repeated, with \(A:\mathcal E'(Y)\to\mathcal D'(X)\).""",r"""A distribution kernel represented by nondegenerate phase charts defines this extension when its wavefront avoids covectors \((x,0;y,\eta)\), \(\eta\ne0\). The other axis condition gives smooth output on smooth test inputs. Merely being a Lagrangian distribution on \(X\times Y\) does not ensure both operator properties.""",r"""This is the combination of \citet[Theorems 2.5.12--2.5.14 and Section 4.1]{hormander1971fourier}, already discussed in 1974-353.8 and 1974-356.10.""",[H])
item(357,'P000593','remark','Pseudodifferential operators as diagonal FIOs',r"""In the PDO case \(\Lambda=\{(x,\xi;x,-\xi)\}\).""",r"""The unprimed kernel relation of a pseudodifferential operator is contained in the nonzero conormal bundle of the diagonal. After reversing the input covector, it becomes the identity relation on \(T^*X\setminus0\). Classical symbols produce the familiar oscillatory kernel with phase \((x-y)\cdot\xi\).""",r"""\citet[Section 4.1]{hormander1971fourier} places pseudodifferential operators in the FIO class. Earlier occurrences include 1971-234.3 and 1973-329.5--7.""",[H],literal='correct')
item(357,'P000593','question','Gelfand asks for a p-adic FIO construction',r"""\ru{Вопрос Г.: хорошо бы в \(p\)-адическом случае построить}. The intended object is the just-discussed calculus.""",r"""The notes request a non-Archimedean analogue without specifying a field, character, symbol class, canonical relations, or closure operations. There is no unique exact theorem to match. A later concrete analogue uses distributional wavefront sets over a \(p\)-adic field and oscillatory integrals with a fixed additive character.""",r"""\citet[Sections 1--2]{heifetz1985padic}, published in 1985, constructs a \(p\)-adic wavefront theory and stationary-phase methods, including pullbacks, products and kernel operations. This is a substantial thematic continuation, but it does not establish that a particular full FIO calculus requested here was completed. No exact solution date is assigned.""",[HE],speaker='I. M. Gelfand',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',status='As of November 2026: relevant non-Archimedean microlocal theories exist, but the notebook prompt is too underspecified to classify as a solved or unsolved definite problem.')
item(357,'P000593','question','When is a distribution kernel a Fourier integral kernel?',r"""Tulovskii answers: wavefront inside a Lagrangian, and transverse exponential tests have an asymptotic expansion determined by that Lagrangian.""",r"""A precise recognition problem is to characterize classical Lagrangian distributions \(I^m_{\mathrm{cl}}(X\times Y,\Lambda)\) by their pairings with localized transverse oscillatory tests, uniformly in smooth phase families and with the order \(m-(\dim X+\dim Y)/4\) prescribed. Wavefront containment alone does not characterize this class; singular amplitudes of arbitrary order can have the same wavefront set.""",r"""\citet[Theorems 3.2.4--3.2.5 and the following paragraph]{hormander1971fourier} already gives the asymptotic test framework and explicitly indicates its converse characterization. The question repeats 1974-356.14 and is expository, with missing uniform symbol bounds rather than an identified research gap.""",[H],match='partial',problem='GP-1974-21')
item(357,'P000593','theorem','Microlocal straightening of a real principal symbol',r"""For an order-one PDO with real \(p\), \(p(x_0,\xi_0)=0\), and \(dp(x_0,\xi_0)\ne0\), there are FIOs \(A,B\) with \(P\equiv B\partial_{X_n}A\) and \(BA\equiv I\) microlocally.""",r"""The standard homogeneous FIO normal form additionally requires that \(H_p\) not be tangent to the radial cone direction at the point. For a classical order-one operator of real principal type under that nonradial condition, elliptic local FIOs quantizing inverse homogeneous canonical transformations conjugate \(P\), modulo smoothing terms, to \(D_{X_n}=-i\partial_{X_n}\), with harmless elliptic factors according to normalization.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 6.1.4 and its application]{duistermaathormander1972fourierii} explicitly includes the nonradial hypothesis. The notebook omits it. The symbol \(p=x\xi\) at \((0,1)\) has \(dp\ne0\) but radial Hamiltonian field, and cannot be straightened to a nonradial coordinate symbol by a homogeneous canonical transformation; see the audit.""",[DH],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status='As of November 2026: the qualified normal-form theorem is established; the dp-only homogeneous formulation is false.')
item(357,'P000593','theorem','Propagation of singularities for real principal type',r"""For \(Pu=f\), \(\mathrm{WF}(u)\setminus\mathrm{WF}(f)\subset\{p=0\}\) and it is invariant under \(H_p=\sum(p_{x_j}\partial_{\xi_j}-p_{\xi_j}\partial_{x_j})\).""",r"""For a scalar classical PDO with real homogeneous principal symbol of principal type, elliptic regularity gives \(\mathrm{WF}(u)\setminus\mathrm{WF}(Pu)\subset\operatorname{Char}(P)\). In the open complement of \(\mathrm{WF}(Pu)\), this set is a union of bicharacteristic segments of the principal Hamilton field. Invariance is understood only while the segment stays in that open region; it does not continue through singular forcing without qualification.""",r"""\citet[Theorem 6.1.1]{duistermaathormander1972fourierii} is the published 1972 result. The notebook's Hamilton field is the negative of the common convention, which reverses orientation but not the unparameterized bicharacteristics.""",[DH],reconstruction='The principal-type scope and restriction to nonsingular forcing are made explicit.')
item(357,'P000594','definition','Bernstein: characteristic variety of an operator ideal',r"""The \(\mathrm{WF}\) of a left ideal of polynomial-coefficient differential operators is the common zero set of its principal symbols.""",r"""Over a characteristic-zero field, let \(D\) be the Weyl algebra and \(I\subsetneq D\) a left ideal. With the order filtration and its induced good quotient filtration, \(\operatorname{Char}(D/I)\subset T^*\mathbb A^n\) is the zero set of \(\operatorname{gr}I\). Symbols of all elements of \(I\), not merely an arbitrary generating list, must be used. The zero section is retained.""",r"""\citet[Section 1, Definition 1.6]{bernstein1971modules} develops characteristic carriers for differential-operator modules. The word \(\mathrm{WF}\) here is an algebraic analogy, not the smooth wavefront of one distribution.""",[B71],speaker='I. N. Bernstein',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(357,'P000594','theorem','Bernstein’s lower bound on characteristic dimension',r"""\(\dim\mathrm{WF}\ge\dim X\).""",r"""For a nonzero finitely generated Weyl-algebra module over a characteristic-zero field, its dimension is at least \(n\). For the order characteristic variety of \(D/I\), this gives \(\dim\operatorname{Char}(D/I)\ge n\). The hypothesis \(I\ne D\) is essential; no corresponding assertion holds for the empty characteristic variety of the zero module.""",r"""\citet[Theorem 1.3, proved in Section 5]{bernstein1972continuation} proves the dimension inequality; the paper was submitted on 21 June 1972 and published in 1972. The 1971 paper supplies the filtered-module framework, not the exact inequality locator. A modern full order-characteristic statement is \citet[Theorem 3.38]{mustata2023dmodules}. Thus the 1975 intervention is expository.""",[B71,B72,'mustata2023dmodules'],speaker='I. N. Bernstein',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
corr('P000593','Normal-form hypothesis',r"""\(p=0,\ dp\ne0\) alone imply the homogeneous FIO normal form.""",r"""Also require the nonradial real-principal-type hypothesis; use \(D_{X_n}=-i\partial_{X_n}\) with the stated Fourier convention.""",r"""The scan lists only \(dp\ne0\). \citet[Proposition 6.1.4]{duistermaathormander1972fourierii} explicitly requires the Hamilton field not to be tangent to the cone axis.""")
corr('P000594','Meaning of Bernstein’s WF','WF of a left differential-operator ideal.','Algebraic characteristic variety of a nonzero quotient module; distinguish it from a distributional wavefront set.','The scan defines a common symbol-zero set, and the dimension bound is Bernstein’s module inequality.','both','provenance')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1974-21',title='Recognizing Lagrangian kernels by oscillatory tests',statement=r"""For a smooth conic Lagrangian \(\Lambda\subset T^*Z\setminus0\), characterize the classical distributions \(I^m_{\mathrm{cl}}(Z,\Lambda)\) by localized pairings against phases transverse to \(\Lambda\), with uniform symbol estimates and prescribed asymptotic order. The stated order and uniformity are reconstructed from the established theory; they are absent from the notebook shorthand.""",occurrences=['1974-356.14','1974-357.4'],status=r"""As of November 2026: established expository question. \citet[Theorems 3.2.4--3.2.5 and following characterization paragraph]{hormander1971fourier} predates both occurrences. The notebook's bare pointwise asymptotic condition is insufficient; this is not an unresolved modern problem.""",status_date='2026-11',refs=[H]))
