talk(358,'1975-03-17','R. L. Dobrushin','P000594-P000598','Random fields specified by conditional laws',r"""The actual talk occupies P000595--P000596. It distinguishes spin, particle, and generalized random fields, formulates the inverse problem of prescribed conditional distributions, then explains regular conditional probabilities and measurable-map disintegration. This returns to the framework needed in Sinai's two talks. The final sentence, about conditioning on countably many real functions, completes the available record.""")
item(358,'P000595','definition','Finite-spin random fields',r"""\(X\) is finite, \(T\) countable, and a random field is a probability measure on \(X^T\); the physical interpretation is a spin system.""",r"""Equip the finite alphabet \(X\) with its discrete sigma-algebra and \(X^T\) with the product sigma-algebra. A probability law there defines the coordinate random variables \(\eta_t\); conversely their consistent finite-dimensional laws determine that probability uniquely. No Gibbs property is part of this definition.""",r"""This is the starting framework of \citet[Section 1]{dobrushin1968conditional}; its use in the notebook is exposition of established probability theory.""",[D],literal='correct')
item(358,'P000595','definition','Point random fields',r"""Realizations are subsets of \(\mathbb R^\nu\) meeting every bounded region in finitely many points; a measure on realizations is a point random field.""",r"""A simple locally finite point field is a probability measure on the configuration space with the count-generated sigma-algebra from 1974-354.1. Local finiteness applies to relatively compact Borel regions; integer-valued counting measures allow multiplicity when desired.""",r"""The continuum configuration framework is explicitly used in \citet[Section 5]{ruelle1970superstable}. This repeats Sinai's model while distinguishing it from a field with a fixed site set.""",[R],literal='correct')
item(358,'P000595','definition','Generalized random fields valued in distributions',r"""The third object is a probability measure on \(\mathcal S^*(\mathbb R^\nu)\), linked to quantum field theory.""",r"""A tempered generalized random field may be represented by a probability measure on \(\mathcal S'(\mathbb R^\nu)\), with evaluation maps \(u\mapsto\langle u,\varphi\rangle\) measurable for every Schwartz test function. Such a field need not admit pointwise values. The mere definition does not construct a measure for an interacting quantum field model.""",r"""\citet[Section 1]{minlos1959generalized} develops generalized random processes and their extension to measures. Its main results were reported on 11 April 1958 and published in 1959, well before this session. The notes offer only the object definition and motivation.""",[MIN],literal='correct')
item(358,'P000596','definition','The Markov-chain comparison',r"""The notes write \(\Pr(x_n\mid x_k,\ k\le n)=\Pr(x_n\mid x_{n-1})\).""",r"""The Markov property is \(\Pr(X_n\in A\mid\sigma(X_k:k<n))=\Pr(X_n\in A\mid X_{n-1})\) almost surely. The conditioning must exclude \(X_n\) itself; conditioning on \(\sigma(X_k:k\le n)\) gives the indicator of \(X_n\in A\), and does not express the usual Markov property.""",r"""The chain analogy in \citet[Section 1]{dobrushin1968conditional} motivates finite-range spatial conditional distributions. The scan clearly has \(k\le n\), so the inequality is a faithful notebook error, not an uncertain transcription.""",[D],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(358,'P000596','problem','Construct all fields with prescribed exterior conditional laws',r"""The problem is to prescribe, for each finite \(V\subset T\), the conditional distribution in \(V\) given the configuration in \(T\setminus V\), then construct the corresponding field measures.""",r"""Given a proper consistent family of probability kernels \((\gamma_V)_{V\Subset T}\) on \(X^T\), determine the set \(\mathcal G(\gamma)=\{\mu:\mu\gamma_V=\mu\text{ for every finite }V\}\), including whether it is nonempty and when it has one element. For finite \(X\) and countable \(T\), quasilocality of a specification suffices for nonemptiness. A contraction condition such as 1974-355.11 gives uniqueness; coexistence shows it is not automatic.""",r"""\citet[Section 1 and Theorems 1--2]{dobrushin1968conditional}, received 20 April 1967 and published in 1968, formulates this exact inverse problem and proves these sufficient existence/uniqueness results. The 1975 statement is an expository programme; it is not a claim that every compatible measurable specification has a universal simple classification.""",[D],problem='GP-1974-20',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',status='As of November 2026: the finite-alphabet quasilocal existence and weak-dependence uniqueness statements are established. The general classification remains a model-dependent programme, not a single unresolved conjecture extracted from this entry.')
item(358,'P000596','definition','Regular conditional probability as a measurable kernel',r"""For a sub-sigma-algebra, conditional probabilities are probability measures depending measurably on the conditioning data and satisfying an integral identity.""",r"""A regular conditional probability of \(\mu\) given \(\mathcal B\subset\mathcal A\) is a probability kernel \(K(\omega,A)\) such that \(K(\cdot,A)\) is \(\mathcal B\)-measurable and \(\int_BK(\omega,A)\,\mu(d\omega)=\mu(A\cap B)\) for every \(A\in\mathcal A,B\in\mathcal B\). For each fixed \(A\), it is a version of \(\mathbb E_\mu[1_A\mid\mathcal B]\).""",r"""This is the kernel form of the canonical systems in \citet[Section 3.1, pp.~125--129]{rokhlin1949measure}. The defining identities are meaningful independently of whether such a probability-valued version exists on an arbitrary space.""",[RO],literal='correct',match='partial')
item(358,'P000596','theorem','Existence and almost-everywhere uniqueness of conditional kernels',r"""In a broad class of cases, regular conditional probabilities exist and are unique.""",r"""For a probability on a standard Borel space and conditioning by a measurable map to another standard Borel space, a disintegration exists and is unique outside a set of conditioning values of pushforward measure zero. In equivalent countably generated conditioning formulations the same almost-everywhere qualification applies. Pointwise uniqueness on null fibres is false.""",r"""\citet[Section 3.1, pp.~125--129]{rokhlin1949measure} proves existence for measurable partitions of Lebesgue spaces and essential uniqueness. Received 15 July 1947, it appeared in Russian in 1949. The notebook's broad-class phrase is correct but supplies neither the space hypotheses nor the null-set qualification.""",[RO],match='partial')
item(358,'P000596','remark','Fubini’s theorem as conditional measure on a rectangle',r"""For the square with planar Lebesgue measure, conditioning on a vertical coordinate gives Lebesgue measure along the corresponding segment.""",r"""On \([0,1]^2\) with product Lebesgue probability and projection \((x,y)\mapsto x\), a disintegration is \(K_x=\delta_x\otimes dy\). Hence \(\int F(x,y)\,dx\,dy=\int(\int F(x,y)\,dy)\,dx\) for integrable \(F\). The explicit kernel can be chosen for every \(x\), although uniqueness is only almost everywhere.""",r"""This is the elementary product example of \citet[Section 3.1]{rokhlin1949measure}; it explains the general-Fubini remark already made by Gelfand in 1974-355.5.""",[RO],literal='correct',match='partial')
item(358,'P000596','construction','Conditioning on a map or a countable family of observables',r"""A measurable \(\xi:X\to F\) defines the conditioning partition; a countable collection of real functions can be treated similarly.""",r"""For standard Borel \(X,F\) and measurable \(\xi\), define \(\nu=\xi_*\mu\). Conditional kernels \(K_y\) satisfy \(\mu(A)=\int_FK_y(A)\,\nu(dy)\) and are concentrated on \(\xi^{-1}(y)\) for \(\nu\)-almost every \(y\). A countable sequence \((\xi_j)\) is a single map into the standard Borel product \(\mathbb R^{\mathbb N}\).""",r"""The measurable partitions generated by one or countably many real functions are explicit examples in \citet[Section 3.1, p.~126]{rokhlin1949measure}. This closes the foundational loop needed for Gibbs exterior conditioning.""",[RO],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1974-20',title='Random fields with prescribed conditional distributions',statement=r"""For a proper consistent specification \((\gamma_V)_{V\Subset T}\) on a finite-alphabet configuration space \(X^T\), determine all probability measures satisfying \(\mu\gamma_V=\mu\) for every finite \(V\), with particular attention to existence and uniqueness.""",occurrences=['1974-355.3','1974-355.7','1974-355.11','1974-358.5'],status=r"""As of November 2026: existence for quasilocal finite-alphabet specifications and uniqueness under Dobrushin contraction are established, as are model-dependent examples of phase coexistence. This is an expository recurrence of \citet[Section 1 and Theorems 1--2]{dobrushin1968conditional}, not a blanket claim that all specifications have been classified.""",status_date='2026-11',refs=[D]))
corr('P000596','Conditioning in the Markov-chain example',r"""\(\Pr(x_n\mid x_k,\ k\le n)\)""",r"""\(\Pr(x_n\mid x_k,\ k<n)\)""",'The scan clearly shows a non-strict inequality. Including the variable being predicted makes its conditional law degenerate; the standard Markov property conditions only on its past.')
