talk(359,'1975-03-31','A. M. Polyakov','P000596-P000599','Spontaneously broken symmetry and particles as field configurations',r"""Only P000597 belongs to the actual session. It contains a title, a particle metaphor, and names: Skyrme, Nielsen, Olesen (written Olsen), Faddeev, Polyakov and 't Hooft. There are no equations or hypotheses from which a specific soliton theorem can be recovered. The final sentence announces Nevanlinna theory following Griffiths for the next meeting.""")
item(359,'P000597','remark','Particle-like structures in theories with broken symmetry',r"""Polyakov discusses spontaneously broken symmetry and describes particles as bubbles in the ether of quantum field theory, followed by a list of researchers.""",r"""The recoverable mathematical theme is that nonlinear field configurations may carry particle-like localized energy and nontrivial topology. The note gives no field space, gauge group, energy functional, boundary conditions, stability criterion, or topological charge, and therefore specifies no particular existence or classification result.""",r"""Polyakov's \citet{polyakov1974particle} is an obvious contemporary primary lead: its 1974 publication predates this talk. Nielsen--Olesen vortices are another distinct lead. The names and metaphor do not license importing either complete model into the notebook. The institutional publication record verifies Polyakov's title and date, but an exact notebook-to-theorem match cannot be established.""",[POL],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026: the broad historical theme is identifiable; the specific mathematical statement and its fate are not. No open problem is created from this three-line record.')
