talk(360,'1975-04-07','James King (attribution marked ? in the notebook)','P000598-P000602','Residue currents, divisors, and the first main theorem',r"""The actual session occupies P000598--P000601; P000602 begins the following dated continuation. The notebook itself places a question mark after King, although its closing bibliographical pointer in the continuation identifies Griffiths--King's Acta paper. The talk passes from logarithmic residues and integration currents to the divisor--Chern-class identity, Bézout, growth of holomorphic maps, and the first main theorem. Its mathematical content is already in \citet[Sections 1 and 5]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}, published in 1973. The raw graph-volume integrals and the integrated Nevanlinna characteristic must be distinguished.""")
item(360,'P000598','remark','Two programmes for holomorphic mapping theory',r"""The opening lists hyperbolic analysis, in which negative curvature restricts holomorphic maps, and counting volumes of divisors by residue calculus.""",r"""These are two announced methods, not two quantified conjectures: differential inequalities for maps into targets with suitably negative curvature, and current identities that compare divisor counting with growth. The first is made precise in record 361; the second is developed in the present talk.""",r"""\citet[Sections 1, 5, and Appendix]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} contains both parts of this programme. The note does not assert that every notion of negative curvature yields the same mapping restriction.""",[GK],literal='correct',match='partial')
item(360,'P000598','construction','Logarithmic residue as integration around a normal circle',r"""The notes write \(\eta=(dg/2\pi ig)\wedge\zeta+\xi\), then \(\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\int_{C(\varepsilon)}\eta=\zeta|_D\), calling \(\eta\) an arbitrary form off \(D=\{g=0\}\).""",r"""If \(D\) is a smooth reduced hypersurface, \(dg\ne0\) along \(D\), and \(\zeta,\xi\) are smooth forms of degrees \(r-1,r\), the displayed logarithmic form has residue \(\zeta|_D\). The limit is integration along the positively oriented normal circle fibres of a shrinking tube. Over an \((r-1)\)-cycle \(c\subset D\), it reads \(\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\int_{\operatorname{tube}_\varepsilon(c)}\eta=\int_c\zeta\). An arbitrary form on the complement need not admit this logarithmic decomposition.""",r"""The local residue/current method is the one used in \citet[Section 1(a), especially Proposition 1.1]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. The tube description is a standard local interpretation of the logarithmic model; the notebook suppresses the smooth-divisor and simple-pole assumptions.""",[GK],reconstruction='Fibre integration distinguishes the form-valued residue from a scalar integral over a whole cycle.',match='partial')
item(360,'P000598','definition','Currents and their distributional exterior derivative',r"""A current is a functional on compactly supported forms; an \(L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}\) form \(\lambda\) gives \([\lambda](\varphi)=\int\lambda\wedge\varphi\), and \(dT(\varphi)=\pm T(d\varphi)\).""",r"""On an oriented real \(2n\)-manifold, a degree-\(r\) current is a continuous linear functional on compactly supported smooth \((2n-r)\)-forms. For a locally integrable \(r\)-form, \([\lambda]\) is defined by integration and \(dT(\varphi)=(-1)^{r+1}T(d\varphi)\). A smooth oriented submanifold defines an integration current of degree its real codimension.""",r"""These conventions are introduced in \citet[Section 1(a)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. The missing continuity and degree convention are supplied; the notebook explicitly leaves the sign open rather than giving a wrong fixed sign.""",[GK],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(360,'P000598','definition','The residue current measures the singular part of differentiation',r"""For a locally integrable form smooth off the exceptional set, \(\operatorname{Res}\eta=d[\eta]-[d\eta]=[\zeta|_D]\).""",r"""Suppose \(\eta\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}\) is smooth off \(D\), and its ordinary derivative there also has a locally integrable extension \(d_{\mathrm{reg}}\eta\). Then \(d[\eta]-[d_{\mathrm{reg}}\eta]\) is supported on \(D\). For the logarithmic model of 1974-360.2 it equals the pushforward \(i_*[\zeta|_D]\), where \(i:D\hookrightarrow V\). The second integrability requirement is necessary to make the difference meaningful in this form.""",r"""The Poincaré--Lelong current calculation of \citet[Proposition 1.1 and its proof]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} gives the model. No assertion that every locally integrable form has a locally integrable ordinary derivative is valid.""",[GK],reconstruction='The notation distinguishes the current derivative from the ordinary derivative on the complement.',match='partial')
item(360,'P000598','method','The one-variable residue normalization',r"""For \(\eta=dz/z\), the notes give \(d[\eta](\varphi)-[\eta](d\varphi)=\lim\int\varphi\,dz=\varphi(0)\).""",r"""With the current convention in 1974-360.3 and counterclockwise circles, \(d[dz/z]=2\pi i\,\delta_0\), while the ordinary derivative of \(dz/z\) is zero off the origin. Equivalently \(d[dz/(2\pi iz)]=\delta_0\). The contour integrand is \(\varphi\,dz/z\). For a degree-one current, \(d[\eta](\varphi)=[\eta](d\varphi)\), so the notebook's written left-hand difference is identically zero.""",r"""This is the elementary one-variable calculation underlying \citet[Proposition 1.1]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. The scan confirms the missing denominator/normalization and the wrong second term; these are faithful notebook errors.""",[GK],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(360,'P000598','theorem','The divisor residue includes vanishing multiplicities',r"""For holomorphic \(g\), \(\operatorname{Res}[dg/(2\pi ig)]=[Z]\), with \(Z=\sum m_iZ_i\) and \(m_i=\operatorname{ord}_{Z_i}g\).""",r"""If \(g\) is a nonzero holomorphic function on a complex manifold, its divisor is \(\operatorname{div}(g)=\sum_i m_iZ_i\). As currents, \(dd^c\log|g|^2=\sum_i m_i[Z_i]\), with \(d^c=(i/4\pi)(\bar\partial-\partial)\). Equivalently the logarithmic differential has this residue current. The weights are part of the divisor; the reduced zero set alone loses them.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 1.1, equation (1.2)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} is an exact Poincaré--Lelong match. The statement includes singular zero sets, via their integration currents.""",[GK],literal='correct')
item(360,'P000599','construction','A divisor determines a line bundle and canonical section',r"""Local equations \(g_i\) for a codimension-one \(X\subset V\) give ratios \(f_{ij}=g_i/g_j\), a bundle \(L_X\), and a section \(\sigma=\{g_i\}\) vanishing on \(X\).""",r"""For an effective Cartier divisor \(D\) on a complex manifold, local equations with invertible holomorphic ratios define \(\mathcal O(D)\) and its canonical holomorphic section \(\sigma\), whose divisor is \(D\). A bare subset of codimension one is insufficient: it must be an analytic divisor with its multiplicities. Frame-transition conventions determine whether one writes the ratio or its reciprocal.""",r"""The divisor and metric setup used by \citet[Sections 0(a) and 1(c)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} is exactly this construction. The notebook suppresses the analytic/Cartier and transition-convention qualifications.""",[GK])
item(360,'P000599','theorem','The Hermitian Poincaré--Lelong identity and its sign',r"""For \(\|\sigma\|^2=h_i|g_i|^2\), the notes put \(\eta=(2\pi i)^{-1}\partial\log\|\sigma\|^2\), identify \([d\eta]\) with \(c(L_D)\), and later write \([D]=d[\eta]+[c(L_D)]\).""",r"""Fix \(d^c=(i/4\pi)(\bar\partial-\partial)\), so \(dd^c=(i/2\pi)\partial\bar\partial\). If \(h=\|e\|^2\) in a holomorphic frame, \(c_1(L,h)=-dd^c\log h\). For a nonzero holomorphic section, \(dd^c\log\|\sigma\|^2=[D]-c_1(L,h)\). Thus the ordinary \(d\eta\) off \(D\), for the notebook's \(\eta\), is \(-c_1(L,h)\), whereas the current derivative has the additional \([D]\) term.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 1.14]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} gives the global current identity. The notebook's two sign assignments are inconsistent under a fixed Chern-form convention; the later \([D]=d[\eta]+c_1\) has the standard sign.""",[GK],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(360,'P000599','theorem','The divisor class is Poincaré dual to the first Chern class',r"""The current de Rham theorem identifies the zero-set current with the Chern class; for closed \(\psi\), \(\int_D\psi=\int_Vc(L_D)\wedge\psi\).""",r"""For a divisor \(D\) on a compact complex \(n\)-manifold, its current cohomology class equals \(c_1(\mathcal O(D))\). Consequently, for a closed smooth \((2n-2)\)-form \(\psi\), \(\int_D\psi=\int_Vc_1(\mathcal O(D),h)\wedge\psi\), counting divisor multiplicities. On a noncompact manifold, take compactly supported test forms or otherwise justify all integrals and boundary terms.""",r"""This is the cohomological interpretation immediately after \citet[Proposition 1.14, pp.~162--163]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. It is already established before the talk.""",[GK])
item(360,'P000599','theorem','Bézout by products of divisor classes',r"""For two curves in \(\mathbb P^2\), the notes identify the number of intersection points with the product of Chern classes and obtain \(d_Xd_Y\).""",r"""For plane projective curves \(X,Y\) over \(\mathbb C\) with no common irreducible component, the sum of local intersection multiplicities is \(\deg X\,\deg Y\). With a hyperplane form \(\omega\) normalized by \(\int_{\mathbb P^2}\omega^2=1\), the cohomological product is \((\deg X)(\deg Y)\int\omega^2\). It counts distinct points only when every intersection is transverse. The product of arbitrary singular currents is not automatically defined; here one uses proper algebraic intersection or an appropriate regularization.""",r"""The divisor-current and proper-intersection formalism of \citet[Section 1(c), Propositions 1.14--1.15]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} supplies the argument. The notebook omits properness and multiplicities in the phrase 'number of points'. This is classical exposition, not a new proof claiming priority.""",[GK],match='partial')
item(360,'P000600','theorem','Integration currents on singular analytic sets',r"""The notes say the theorem remains valid for \(X\) with singularities, followed by 'Lelong, 1953'. """,r"""For a pure-dimensional analytic set \(X\), integration over its regular locus has locally finite mass and extends to a closed positive current \([X]\) on the ambient manifold. Analytic cycles are handled by summing these currents with their multiplicities. This justifies the divisor-current formulas even when the divisor is singular; it does not define arbitrary products of such currents.""",r"""\citet[Theorems 7--8, pp.~257--258]{lelong1957integration} proves the integration-current extension and additivity; the paper is from 1957. Its bibliography includes a 1953 Lelong contribution, but the notebook's precise 1953 attribution was not verified. The checked 1957 publication is sufficient to establish that the result predates the seminar.""",[LE,GK],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(360,'P000600','construction','Pullback volume counts the multiplicity of a holomorphic map',r"""For \(f:\mathbb C^n\to V\) with compact Kähler target, \(\operatorname{vol}(f(B(r)))=\int_{B(r)}f^*(\omega^n)\).""",r"""The integral \(n!^{-1}\int_{B(r)}f^*\omega^n\) is the parametrized \(2n\)-dimensional volume of \(f\), counting local covering multiplicity. It equals the ordinary image volume only under suitable almost-everywhere injectivity; it vanishes where the complex rank is below \(n\). The factor \(n!\) is omitted if volume is defined using \(\omega^n\) itself.""",r"""\citet[Section 5(a)--(b)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} uses these pullback growth integrals. The notebook's equality needs the multiplicity interpretation; it is not an identity for unweighted images of arbitrary maps.""",[GK],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(360,'P000600','construction','Graph volume and the mixed raw growth integrals',r"""For the graph above \(B(r)\), the notes expand \(\int_{\Gamma(r)}(\varphi+\omega)^n\) and define \(T_k(f,r)=\int_{B(r)}\varphi^{n-k}\wedge f^*(\omega^k)\).""",r"""Let \(\omega_0\) be the Euclidean Kähler form and put \(t_k(f,r)=\int_{B(r)}\omega_0^{n-k}\wedge f^*\omega^k\), \(0\le k\le n\). Then \(n!\operatorname{Vol}(\Gamma_f|_{B(r)})=\sum_{k=0}^n\binom nk t_k(f,r)\) for the product metric. These are raw ball integrals; a Nevanlinna characteristic is obtained by an additional radial integration and normalization.""",r"""The graph-growth method and higher characteristic forms are discussed in \citet[Section 5, pp.~180--183]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. Changing the notebook's raw \(T_k\) to \(t_k\) here prevents confusion with the integrated characteristic on the next page.""",[GK],reconstruction='The lower-case notation is editorial, explicitly separating two quantities denoted T in the notebook.',literal='correct',match='partial')
item(360,'P000600','claim','The unspecified comparison of higher growth with first growth',r"""The notes say 'in many cases' \(T_k=O(T_1)\), adding 'of one order'. """,r"""No family of maps, target hypothesis, range of \(k\), or choice between raw and integrated characteristics is specified. Therefore the phrase gives no definite universal estimate. The graph-volume identity by itself does not imply domination of every higher mixed integral by the first.""",r"""\citet[Section 5(b), pp.~182--183]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} discusses the different higher growth quantities and the difficulty of reducing their information to first growth. This is thematic support for the surrounding subject, not verification of an unqualified \(O\)-estimate. No exact source or modern open question is assigned to 'many cases'. """,[GK],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low',status='As of November 2026: the intended restricted comparison is not identifiable; it is not classified as an undeveloped general theorem.')
item(360,'P000600','construction','Pulling a divisor identity back by a holomorphic map',r"""For a positive line bundle with \(c(L)=\omega\) and \(D=\{\sigma=0\}\), the notes write \(d[\eta]-[\omega]=[D]\) and the same formula after pullback by \(f\).""",r"""Let \(L\to V\) have a smooth Hermitian metric with positive curvature form \(\omega=c_1(L,h)\), and let \(f:\mathbb C^n\to V\) have image not contained in the zero divisor \(D\) of \(\sigma\). The pullback section \(f^*\sigma\) defines \(D_f=f^*D\), with multiplicity, and \(dd^c\log\|f^*\sigma\|^2=[D_f]-f^*\omega\). Thus \([D_f]=dd^c\log\|f^*\sigma\|^2+f^*\omega\). This is justified by applying Poincaré--Lelong to the pullback section, not by assuming that every current has a pullback under every map.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 1.14 and Section 5(b)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} gives precisely the identity used in the first main theorem. The minus sign in the notebook's rearranged formula conflicts with its correct formula on P000599.""",[GK],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(360,'P000600','method','The missing boundary term in the counting calculation',r"""After multiplication by \(\chi_{B(r)}\varphi^{n-1}\), a displayed integral over apparently \(S(r)\) of \(\varphi^{n-1}\wedge f^*\omega\) is equated to divisor volume.""",r"""For almost every radius, the current identity gives \(\int_{D_f\cap B(r)}\omega_0^{n-1}=\int_{B(r)}f^*\omega\wedge\omega_0^{n-1}+\int_{S(r)}d^c\log\|f^*\sigma\|^2\wedge\omega_0^{n-1}\), interpreted by smooth approximation if needed. The first integral is over the ball, while the boundary integral has degree \(2n-1\) and contains the logarithmic derivative. Integrating the \(2n\)-form \(f^*\omega\wedge\omega_0^{n-1}\) over a sphere is dimensionally inappropriate.""",r"""This is the Stokes step in the twice-integrated Poincaré--Lelong argument of \citet[Proposition 5.11 and its reference to Section 3]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna}. The notebook has suppressed the essential boundary term; the faint domain letter does not repair that omission.""",[GK],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The sign follows the fixed ddc convention; the stated identity supplies the missing Stokes term.')
item(360,'P000600-P000601','definition','Integrated divisor counting and the radius exponent',r"""The notes pass from raw divisor volume to \(N(D_f,r)=\int_0^r(\int_{D_f\cap B(\rho)}\varphi^{n-1})\,d\rho/\rho^{n+1}\), while the preceding line uses the classical \(d\rho/\rho\) notation.""",r"""For ordinary Euclidean radius and \(\omega_0\) fixed, set \(n_D(t)=\int_{D_f\cap B(t)}\omega_0^{n-1}\). Up to one common positive dimensional normalization, \(N_f(D,r)=c_n\int_{r_0}^r n_D(t)t^{-(2n-1)}\,dt\), where \(r_0>0\). Define \(T_f(r)=c_n\int_{r_0}^r(\int_{B(t)}f^*\omega\wedge\omega_0^{n-1})t^{-(2n-1)}\,dt\), using the same Jensen normalization \(c_n\). For \(n=1\), the weight is \(dt/t\). Equivalently one first normalizes the raw masses by \(t^{2n-2}\) and then integrates with \(dt/t\).""",r"""The counting and characteristic definitions used in \citet[Sections 3(a) and 5(b)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} have the Jensen radial normalization. The notebook's \(\rho^{n+1}\) is not the Euclidean-radius exponent \(2n-1\) (they happen to agree at \(n=2\)); it already fails the one-dimensional check.""",[GK],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='A fixed positive base radius avoids origin conventions; the shared dimensional constant is left explicit rather than importing a hidden normalization.')
item(360,'P000601','theorem','The first main theorem bounds divisor counting by growth',r"""The final inequality is \(N(D_f,r)\le T(f,r)+O(1)\), called the first Nevanlinna inequality.""",r"""For the compact Hermitian target and nonzero pullback section above, scale \(\sigma\) so that \(\|\sigma\|\le1\). With compatible Jensen normalizations, the nonnegative proximity term \(m_f(D,r)\), the spherical average of \(\log(1/\|f^*\sigma\|)\) with the corresponding factor, satisfies \(N_f(D,r)+m_f(D,r)=T_f(r)+O(1)\). Hence \(N_f(D,r)\le T_f(r)+O(1)\). The bounded term depends on the divisor, metric, and initial-radius normalization, but not on \(r\).""",r"""\citet[Proposition 5.11 and equation (5.12), p.~184]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} is the exact source, published in 1973, two years before this talk. This is an established first main theorem; no second-main-theorem or defect assertion is silently added. It connects thematically with the earlier Picard/Nevanlinna discussion 1965-71.1.""",[GK],literal='correct')
corr('P000598','Example with dz/z',r"""The current difference is \(d[\eta](\varphi)-[\eta](d\varphi)\); the contour has \(\varphi\,dz\), and the answer is \(\varphi(0)\).""",r"""Use \(d[\eta]-[d_{\mathrm{reg}}\eta]\), contour \(\varphi\,dz/z\), and \(2\pi i\varphi(0)\), or normalize \(\eta=dz/(2\pi iz)\).""",'The scan confirms these symbols. With degree-one current signs the written difference vanishes; the positively oriented contour computes the stated corrected factor.')
