item(361,'P000602','theorem','The first main inequality recalled with its constant suppressed',r"""The continuation repeats \(N(D_f,r)\le T(f,r)\) and labels an iterated integral the order of growth; its inner ball is written \(B(r)\) although the outer variable is \(t\).""",r"""Under the hypotheses and compatible counting definitions of 1974-360.17--18, the invariant statement is \(N_f(D,r)\le T_f(r)+O(1)\). A chosen additive normalization of \(T_f\) may absorb the bounded term for a fixed divisor, but the term cannot simply be dropped for arbitrary normalizations. The inner ball in a radial growth integral is \(B(t)\), and raw ball masses require the dimension-dependent normalization described there.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 5.11 and equation (5.12)]{griffithsking1973nevanlinna} proves the recalled result. This is a second occurrence of the same established first main theorem, not a new strengthened inequality.""",[GK],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(361,'P000602','definition','The sign and normalization of the Ricci form of a volume',r"""First \(\operatorname{Ric}\Omega=i\partial\bar\partial\log g\) for \(\Omega=(i/2)g\,dz\wedge d\bar z\); then the general formula has \(i/(2\pi)\). The text says its scalar coefficient is minus Gaussian curvature.""",r"""For a positive smooth volume form locally \(\Omega=a\,dV_{\mathrm{hol}}\), define \(R_\Omega=dd^c\log a=(i/2\pi)\partial\bar\partial\log a\). The form is invariant under holomorphic coordinate changes, because the logarithm of the Jacobian modulus squared is pluriharmonic. This is the negative of the usual normalized Ricci form. In one complex dimension, with metric area \(\Omega=(i/2)g\,dz\wedge d\bar z\) and Gaussian curvature \(K_g\), \(i\partial\bar\partial\log g=-K_g\Omega\), while \(R_\Omega=-K_g\Omega/(2\pi)\).""",r"""The volume-form curvature convention and coordinate invariance are used in \citet[Section 2]{griffiths1971canonical}. The notebook has the correct negative-Gaussian sign, but shifts the normalization by \(2\pi\) between two displays; those displays must not be combined without that factor.""",[G],match='partial',reconstruction='The notation R is editorial and makes explicit that the notebook uses the negative Ricci convention.')
item(361,'P000602','definition','The volume-curvature inequalities needed for comparison',r"""The two explicit assumptions are \(\operatorname{Ric}\Omega>0\) and \((\operatorname{Ric}\Omega)^n\ge\Omega\).""",r"""On a complex \(n\)-manifold with a positive \(C^2\) volume form \(\Omega\), assume \(R_\Omega\) is a positive definite real \((1,1)\)-form and \(R_\Omega^n\ge\Omega\) as positive top-degree forms, in the same \(dd^c\) convention. This determinant inequality fixes the necessary scale. Multiplication of \(\Omega\) by a positive constant leaves \(R_\Omega\) unchanged but changes the comparison inequality.""",r"""\citet[Lemma 2.3 and the proof of Proposition 2.7]{griffiths1971canonical} supplies volume forms with such normalized curvature control in the canonical algebraic setting and the corresponding maximum-principle argument. The notebook's explicit analytic inequalities are more informative than its later shorthand 'negative curvature'. """,[G],literal='correct',match='partial')
item(361,'P000602','theorem','Ahlfors-type volume decrease from the ball or polydisc',r"""With the Bergman volume on \(B^n\) or \(\Delta^n\), every holomorphic map to \(V^n\) with negatively curved volume satisfies \(f^*\Omega_V\le\Phi_B\).""",r"""Normalize the complete invariant volume \(\Phi_U\) on the unit ball or polydisc \(U\) so that \(R_{\Phi_U}>0\) and \(R_{\Phi_U}^n=\Phi_U\). If a positive \(C^2\) volume form \(\Omega_V\) satisfies \(R_{\Omega_V}>0\) and \(R_{\Omega_V}^n\ge\Omega_V\), every holomorphic \(f:U\to V\) satisfies \(f^*\Omega_V\le\Phi_U\). At rank-deficient points the left side vanishes. The comparison follows by applying the maximum principle to the ratio \(f^*\Omega_V/\Phi_{U_s}\) on smaller balls or polydiscs \(U_s\) and letting \(s\) increase to one.""",r"""\citet[Proposition 2.7, pp.~444--445]{griffiths1971canonical}, received 27 March 1970 and published in May 1971, proves volume decrease for maps to complete canonical algebraic manifolds. Its proof gives the displayed analytic sufficient-condition version. The notebook's blanket curvature-\(-1\) description must be replaced by compatible source and target volume normalizations; the polydisc does not have constant holomorphic sectional curvature.""",[G],reconstruction='The analytic curvature inequalities explicitly stated in the notes are used as hypotheses; the checked publication has the canonical-algebraic target as its stated setting. This is a faithful proof reconstruction, not a claim of a new generalization.',match='partial')
